WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.810
Bitcoin is the king of cryptocurrency.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:08.900
Since the creation of Bitcoin, we have seen
dozens of altcoins hit the market such as

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.690
Cardano, Ethereum, and of course DogeCoin.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.190
Despite these introductions though, there
has been little doubt that Bitcoin has always

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:17.360
remained the leader.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.240
But, when you consider the merits of ethereum
and the shortfalls of bitcoin, Bitcoin’s

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.280
dominance isn’t nearly as clear over the
long term.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.410
So, here’s why Ethereum will squash Bitcoin.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:34.719
Before we get into the merits of ethereum,
let’s first take a look at the origins of

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:39.350
Ethereum, as this will tell us a lot about
the future potential of Ethereum.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.550
Ethereum was founded by Vitalik Buterin and
Gavin Wood in 2015.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:47.780
Vitalik had been in the crypto space since
his teenage years and he had even co-founded

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.960
bitcoin magazine in 2012.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.251
Though he loved blockchain technology and
the potential of decentralized finance, he

00:00:54.251 --> 00:00:56.629
had one major gripe with bitcoin.

00:00:56.629 --> 00:01:00.319
He says that people in the bitcoin community
“weren’t approaching the problem in the

00:01:00.319 --> 00:01:01.530
right way.”

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:06.189
According to Vitalik, it seemed like the bitcoin
community was targeting individual applications

00:01:06.189 --> 00:01:08.549
as opposed to blockchain technology as a whole.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:14.350
So, Vitalyk would start pitching his vision
for a better cryptocurrency in 2013.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:19.570
Just a year later, Vitalyk and Gavin would
crowdfund $18 million to develop Ethereum,

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:21.520
and we would see Ethereum hit the market in
2015.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.259
Fun fact, Vitalik was just 21 years old when
he launched Ethereum.

00:01:26.259 --> 00:01:31.979
Anyway, a common misconception is that Ethereum
is another cryptocurrency just like Bitcoin,

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:34.909
but this is actually not true.

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:38.329
Ethereum is actually just a platform/technology.

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:43.109
One cryptocurrency that is built on this platform
is Ether, and Ether is actually what people

00:01:43.109 --> 00:01:45.909
are investing into when they say they bought
ethereum.

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:51.030
As you can see, Ether was not developed as
a me too coin or as a quick cash grab.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:56.070
No, Ether was developed on the Ethereum platform
which was meticulously designed to fix the

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.329
flaws of Bitcoin.

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:02.840
So, it’s not surprising to see that Ethereum
is far stronger than Bitcoin in several aspects

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.049
such as utility.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:09.380
Bitcoin is often described to be digital gold,
and that’s a pretty accurate description.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.760
Like gold, you don’t really do much with
bitcoin.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.370
Some people argue that bitcoin can be used
for transactions, but no one buying bitcoin

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.050
is really looking to spend it, at least not
in the short term.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:25.190
People investing into bitcoin are either looking
to make some quick cash in the bull run or

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:26.980
they’re looking to hold for decades.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:32.540
So, like gold, Bitcoin is really just a store
of value, albeit a rather voltalite one at

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.100
this point.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:38.360
Ethereum on the other hand has several use
cases such as smart contracts.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:42.510
Smart contracts are basically just regular
contracts, but the overseeing entity is just

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:43.960
lines of code.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:47.700
Right now, we have plenty of overseeing authorities
that make sure that all parties involved in

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.600
a contract are fairly treated, and that the
terms of the contract are met.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.630
A great example of a real life contract is
paying taxes.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:03:00.680
We pay taxes to the government, and the government
ideally uses that money to better our lives.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.840
In the meantime, there are several overseeing
authorities that make sure everything runs

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:05.840
smoothly.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.010
For instance, if someone doesn’t pay their
taxes, they may be arrested by the police

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:13.190
or raided by the FBI in extreme situations.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:17.270
In this scenario, the police and FBI are the
overseeing authorities.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:22.550
Similarly, if the government abuses their
power and spends money in wasteful ways, citizens

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:26.100
have the right to sue the government through
the courts in which case the courts are the

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.670
overseeing authority.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:32.770
The problem with this system is that the overseeing
authority is inefficient and/or biased.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:36.680
Smart contracts aim to eliminate this issue
by making the overseeing authorities blockchain

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:37.950
technology.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.300
When a smart contract is created, lines of
code are written that describe the various

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.180
terms of the contract.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.680
The blockchain will automatically enforce
the terms of the contract and ensure that

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.890
all parties are fairly served.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:52.670
Going back to the tax example, the blockchain
would keep track of all the money you make

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:56.550
in a year, and it will automatically calculate
and deduct the appropriate amount from your

00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:00.450
crypto wallet at the end of the year and transfer
it to the IRS.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:05.420
As you can see, this technology is extremely
useful within the financial industry.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:11.100
Everything from taxes, loans, insurance, purchases,
securities trading, and basically anything

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.540
that involves transactions can benefit from
smart contracts.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:18.540
And right now, Ethereum is the primary platform
capable of this service.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:23.120
Aside from smart contracts, Ethereum also
allows anyone to create programs on the Ethereum

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:24.130
platform that would benefit from blockchain
technology and Ether.

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:28.480
These programs are called decentralized applications
and one of the most popular decentralized

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.560
applications is NFTs.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.030
NFTs are built on the Ethereum platform and
allow individuals to buy digital files such

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.500
as images, videos, and audio.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:43.640
Now, I’m not quite convinced about the usefulness
of NFTS and I know a lot of you guys aren’t

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:44.640
either.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:49.950
But that doesn’t really matter as NFTs are
just an example of a decentralized application.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.190
A more practical application may be a decentralized
social media platform.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:58.270
Social media giants like Facebook, Google,
and Twitter not only have full control over

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:02.940
what is posted on their platforms, but they
also have heaps of information stored about

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:04.120
all of us.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.590
This makes many of us uncomfortable for obvious
reasons.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.780
With a decentralized social media platform,
not only would any one entity not be able

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:16.010
to censor certain information, but there also
wouldn’t be a corporate giant tracking each

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.680
and every one of our posts.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.560
There’s millions upon millions of use cases
for decentralized applications, and like with

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.450
smart contracts, Ethereum is the leader by
far within this space as well.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:27.450
So, ethereum not only offers a cryptocurrency
like Bitcoin, but it also offers an entire

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:28.450
platform for smart contracts and decentralized
applications giving Ether and Ethereum much

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:29.450
more utility.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:32.850
Aside from being much more useful, Ethereum
is also much more eco friendly.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:37.041
I think you guys probably already heard about
Elon’s tweet that suggests that Bitcoin

00:05:37.041 --> 00:05:42.740
mining burns large amounts of coal and that
Bitcoin is therefore environmentally unfriendly.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:46.680
People were quick to point out that 70% of
bitcoin mining is done using renewable energy.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:50.970
But, I don’t think this is what Elon Musk
was really referring to when he bashed on

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:52.340
bitcoin mining.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:57.040
I think his main point was that Bitcoin’s
energy/transaction is unfavorable which he

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.600
points out at the end of the tweet saying
quote “We are also looking at other cryptocurrencies

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.550
that use &lt;1% of Bitcoin’s energy/transaction.”

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:09.880
Even if bitcoin was only mined with renewable
energy, the energy/transaction would still

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:12.940
be just as unfavorable as Elon points out.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:17.950
Ether, however, is far more efficient when
it comes to energy/transaction because unlike

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:22.690
bitcoin which uses a proof of work model,
ethereum is shifting to use a proof of stake

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:23.980
model.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:28.270
Most of you guys are likely unfamiliar with
both of these models, so let's take a deeper

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:29.820
look at each of them.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.651
Bitcoin mining as well as traditional ethereum
mining rely on the proof of work model, and

00:06:34.651 --> 00:06:38.830
I think the best way to explain this model
is through an example with iPhones.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:42.360
Let's say that you and a friend work at an
iPhone repair shop.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:46.040
This repair shop specializes in unlocking
bricked iPhones.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:51.139
All of the iPhones at this shop have a 4 digit
passcode and unlimited attempts, but neither

00:06:51.139 --> 00:06:53.290
of you know what the passcode is.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:57.380
The owner of the shop, however, knows what
all the passcodes are, and he wants you guys

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:01.800
to try all of the combinations and see if
you arrive at the same passcode as him.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:05.210
For every passcode you confirm with him, he
promises to pay you $10.

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:10.100
You’re a pretty fast typer, and you’re
able to test out 100 passcodes every single

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:11.100
minute.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:14.870
Given that there are 10,000 total combinations,
it takes you 5000 attempts on average to crack

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:16.010
the passcode.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:21.260
At 100 passcodes per minute, you end up confirming
a passcode once every 50 minutes meaning that

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.480
you earn about $12 per hour.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:28.430
Your friend on the other hand is an extremely
fast typer and he’s able to test out 1000

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:29.430
passcodes every minute.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:35.500
At that rate, he’s able to confirm a passcode
once every 5 minutes earning himself $120

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:36.880
every single hour.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:41.320
In a proof of work model, this is exactly
what miners are doing, but at a much larger

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:42.400
scale.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:48.100
Whenever a bitcoin transaction occurs, a unique
64 digit hexadecimal number is created.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:52.460
Miners are given the task of guessing and
confirming this number, and whoever verifies

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:55.390
the transaction first is rewarded some bitcoin.

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:58.760
As you can see though, this is an extremely
inefficient process.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:05.330
When you’re guessing iPhone passcodes, on
average you waste 4999 tries on every single

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:06.330
iPhone.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:12.550
This means that 99.98% of your attempts are
a complete waste of time, energy, and resources.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:17.490
This inefficiency gives bitcoin a very high
energy consumption per transaction.

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:21.940
Ethereum has also used this model for basically
their entire lifetime, but they are currently

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:25.729
in the process of switching to proof of stake,
and they expect the transition to be completed

00:08:25.729 --> 00:08:27.690
by the end of 2021.

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:32.879
In a proof of stake model, everything is exactly
the same except for the brute force part.

00:08:32.879 --> 00:08:36.419
Going back to the iPhone example, the owner
of the iPhone shop recognizes that having

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:40.059
both of you brute force the passcodes is extremely
inefficient.

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:44.569
As a result he decides to tell you the passcode,
and he just wants you to enter the passcode

00:08:44.569 --> 00:08:47.389
and make sure that it really is the right
passcode.

00:08:47.389 --> 00:08:51.040
He tells either of you the passcode of an
iPhone based on how much he can trust both

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:52.100
of you.

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:55.619
His trust in you is based on how long you
have worked at the shop and how much equity

00:08:55.619 --> 00:08:56.839
you own in the shop.

00:08:56.839 --> 00:09:00.709
Let’s say both of you have been working
at the shop for the same amount of time, but

00:09:00.709 --> 00:09:04.920
you only own 1% of the shop while your friend
owns 10% of the shop.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:09.319
In this scenario, the shop owner trusts your
friend 10 times more than you.

00:09:09.319 --> 00:09:13.959
So, he gives your friend 10 times the number
of phones to verify as he gives you.

00:09:13.959 --> 00:09:18.160
If one of you guys lie about a verification
or try to trick the shop owner in any manner,

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:21.670
you’ll lose a portion of your stake in the
business based on how malicious your action

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:22.670
was.

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:26.540
In terms of the Ethereum universe, the trust
you have is based on how much ether you own

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:28.060
and how long you have owned your ether.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:32.860
You need to have a minimum of 32 ether to
become a staker, and if you decide to become

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:37.550
a staker, the network will give you transactions
to verify based on your credibility.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:41.230
If you don’t fulfill your responsibility
of verifying these transactions or you try

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:45.540
to hack the network or something, you will
lose portions or all of your ether.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:50.749
In a proof of stake model, you’re not wasting
any computing power, so the energy/transaction

00:09:50.749 --> 00:09:55.389
is oftentimes hundreds if not thousands of
times less than a proof of work model.

00:09:55.389 --> 00:09:59.410
But wait a minute, couldn’t bitcoin just
shift to a proof of stake model?

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:04.360
Well, it definitely could and experts predict
that it will eventually happen.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:08.860
However, a lot of the bitcoin community is
extremely reluctant to make the switch.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:12.709
They claim that the proof of stake model is
less secure than the proof of work model.

00:10:12.709 --> 00:10:17.139
But, several altcoins have been using the
proof of stake model for years at this point,

00:10:17.139 --> 00:10:19.529
and there has been no evidence of less security.

00:10:19.529 --> 00:10:23.930
If anything, proof of stake is even more secure
as the people verifying transactions have

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:28.529
a strong incentive to keep the given crypto
strong as they hold large amounts of it.

00:10:28.529 --> 00:10:33.679
Moreover, proof of work models have their
own security risks such as being 51% attacked.

00:10:33.679 --> 00:10:37.260
I think the primary reason a large part of
the bitcoin community is against the proof

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:42.269
of stake model is because they already invested
large amounts of money into mining equipment.

00:10:42.269 --> 00:10:46.040
And that computing power would basically become
useless if bitcoin transitions to proof of

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:47.110
stake.

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:51.929
Nonetheless, the general consensus is that
bitcoin will eventually move to proof of stake

00:10:51.929 --> 00:10:54.170
and that proof of stake is the future.

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:57.920
This means that Ether is the largest crypto
supporting this significantly more efficient

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.209
verification method.

00:10:59.209 --> 00:11:05.550
So, ethereum not only offers more utility,
but it has also proven to be much more flexible.

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:10.069
Despite all of these advantages, for me, Bitcoin
has always been the better option for one

00:11:10.069 --> 00:11:15.089
reason which is that Bitcoin has a limited
supply while Ether does not.

00:11:15.089 --> 00:11:18.589
Likely one of the largest problems with fiat
currency is that governments keep printing

00:11:18.589 --> 00:11:21.959
more of it which continuously devalues the
currency.

00:11:21.959 --> 00:11:25.959
And one of the main appeals of bitcoin is
that it has limited supply.

00:11:25.959 --> 00:11:29.500
But as I looked deeper into this, my mind
was changed.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:33.480
Here’s the thing, though bitcoin has a limited
supply, the last bitcoin isn’t expected

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:35.300
to be mined till 2140.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:39.199
So, bitcoin will be inflating for the entirety
of our lives.

00:11:39.199 --> 00:11:43.829
More importantly though, Ether will actually
have a lower inflation rate than Bitcoin.

00:11:43.829 --> 00:11:48.240
The bitcoin inflation rate is currently 1.76%
per year.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:51.930
Once Ether completes the switch to proof of
stake though, its inflation rate is expected

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:53.850
to be less than 1%.

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:55.720
This isn’t just an accident either.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:59.089
Since the creation of Ether, the developers
have been keen to keep the inflation rate

00:11:59.089 --> 00:12:02.149
of Ether comparable to Bitcoin if not less.

00:12:02.149 --> 00:12:07.139
So, despite having unlimited supply, Ether
is actually just as deflationary as Bitcoin

00:12:07.139 --> 00:12:08.339
if not more.

00:12:08.339 --> 00:12:13.269
And that brings me onto my final point which
is that if Bitcoin is the digital gold, Ethereum

00:12:13.269 --> 00:12:15.110
is the digital oil.

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:18.629
Every year, 35 billion barrels of oil are
consumed.

00:12:18.629 --> 00:12:23.389
Right now, the cost per barrel of oil is about
$60 and that’s actually about the average

00:12:23.389 --> 00:12:25.310
over the last 50 years.

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:30.110
This means that roughly $2.1 trillion worth
of oil is consumed every year.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:33.860
Meanwhile, the entire market cap of gold is
only $11 trillion.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:38.119
And, that’s not even taking into account
the industries that oil makes possible such

00:12:38.119 --> 00:12:43.019
as the automobile industry and the aviation
industry and agriculture industry and the

00:12:43.019 --> 00:12:45.360
plastic industry and countless more.

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:49.640
Oil accounts for tens of trillions of dollars
of economic output every single year, and

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:53.639
I believe that this is the role Ethereum will
play in the larger global economy in terms

00:12:53.639 --> 00:12:55.380
of blockchain technology.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:58.250
When will Ethereum actually take over bitcoin
then?

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:03.190
Well, I don’t think the price per ether
will ever surpass Bitcoin; however, I do believe

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:06.940
that Ether’s market cap will eventually
surpass Bitcoin’s market cap.

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:10.639
Right now, Ether’s market cap is about 40%
of that of Bitcoin.

00:13:10.639 --> 00:13:16.110
But, we are experiencing a massive crash right
now/potentially entering a bear market.

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:21.660
At the peak of the last bull cycle, Ether’s
market cap peaked at 72% of that of Bitcoin.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:26.370
If we do have another pump this cycle, it’s
very possible that Ether reaches 80 or 90%

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:31.209
of Bitcoin’s market cap or it might even
barely surpass bitcoin’s market cap.

00:13:31.209 --> 00:13:35.639
However, I don’t think Ether will be able
to sustain the lead at this point in time.

00:13:35.639 --> 00:13:39.329
During the next bear market, Ether will likely
end up falling far greater than bitcoin in

00:13:39.329 --> 00:13:41.660
terms of percentage once again.

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:45.790
In the 2025 bull market though, there’s
a high probability in my opinion that Ether

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:48.740
at least momentarily overtakes bitcoin.

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:52.929
And by the 2030s, I think people will start
to realize the massive utilitarian potential

00:13:52.929 --> 00:13:58.319
of Ethereum and that Ether will sustainably
overtake Bitcoin and become the new king of

00:13:58.319 --> 00:13:59.319
crypto.

00:13:59.319 --> 00:14:03.920
Again, this isn’t to say that Bitcoin will
fail, but simply that Ether will be even bigger

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:04.920
than Bitcoin.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:08.110
If you’re still not convinced, just take
a look at history.

00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:11.949
The pioneer of an industry is almost never
the final victor.

00:14:11.949 --> 00:14:15.819
Ford was the first to introduce mass production
automobiles, but General Motors ended up being

00:14:15.819 --> 00:14:17.239
the bigger automobile company.

00:14:17.239 --> 00:14:22.249
AOL and Yahoo seemed to be the winners of
the internet in the 1990s, but we now know

00:14:22.249 --> 00:14:24.589
that that title goes to Google.

00:14:24.589 --> 00:14:30.230
IBM introduced the first smartphone in the
world in 1994, but now, no one even knows

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:31.749
they made a smartphone.

